<usx version="3.1.2">
  <book code="ECC" style="id">EN_UST en_English_ltr Thu Dec 17 2020 21:50:24 GMT-0600 (Central Standard Time) tc</book>
  <para style="ide">UTF-8
</para>
  <para style="h">Ecclesiastes
</para>
  <para style="toc1">The Book of Ecclesiastes
</para>
  <para style="toc2">Ecclesiastes
</para>
  <para style="toc3">Ecc
</para>
  <para style="mt1">Ecclesiastes

</para>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <chapter number="1" style="c" sid="ECC 1"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 1:1"/>The following is the written lesson of Qoheleth, who was a descendant of David and who ruled as king in Jerusalem.
<verse eid="ECC 1:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 1:2"/>Qoheleth said:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 1:2">All of human life is as temporary 
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:2">as the mist of my breath!
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:2">Everything is emptiness!

<verse eid="ECC 1:2"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 1:3"/>I want to know whether the never-ending labor of being alive in this world
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:3">ever produces any sense of satisfaction or joy in this life
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:3">that makes the labor of life worthwhile.

<verse eid="ECC 1:3"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 1:4"/>One generation of people dies, and another generation is born.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:4">Nonetheless, nothing seems to change.
<verse eid="ECC 1:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 1:5"/>The sun rises every morning and sets each evening.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:5">Then, it hurries back to the eastern horizon,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:5">from which it will  rise again.
<verse eid="ECC 1:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 1:6"/>The wind blows southward.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:6">Then, it changes direction and blows northward.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 1:6">It seems to go around and around,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:6">and yet, it always seems to go where it is supposed to go.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 1:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 1:7"/>All rivers and streams flow into the ocean,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:7">but the ocean is never full.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 1:7">Instead, all that water will continue to flow
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:7">wherever it has always flowed.
<verse eid="ECC 1:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 1:8"/>These  natural processes
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:8">make humanity so indescribably tired.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 1:8">Similarly, no matter how much people have seen,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:8">they will always want to see more;
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 1:8">no matter how much people have heard,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:8">they will always want to hear more.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 1:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 1:9"/>Whatever happened in the past will happen again.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:9">Likewise, whatever people have done in the past, people will do it again.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:9">Nothing happens that is ever new.
<verse eid="ECC 1:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 1:10"/>Now, someone might claim
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:10">to have discovered something  novel.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 1:10">But, in reality someone discovered it a long time ago,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:10">in times long since past.
<verse eid="ECC 1:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 1:11"/>No one ever remembers the people or the events of the past.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:11">The same will be true of the people and events of the future,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:11">and even of those after that!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 1:11"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 1:12"/>I, Qoheleth, ruled as the king of Israel in the city of Jerusalem.
<verse eid="ECC 1:12"/><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 1:13"/>I resolved within myself to use my intellect and my understanding of the world to study everything that people do during their lives on this earth. I discovered that God has employed every human being in the difficult job of being alive.
<verse eid="ECC 1:13"/><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 1:14"/>Yes, I observed everything that people do during their lives. Now pay attention! I saw that our lives are as temporary as the mist of my breath. Humanity’s labor is often as silly as trying to catch the wind.

<verse eid="ECC 1:14"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 1:15"/>It is just like people say:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 1:15">“No one can fix what appears broken,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:15">and no one can count what is not there.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 1:15"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 1:16"/>So, I said to myself, “Now pay attention! You know that I, Qoheleth, have become wiser than any other king who has ever reigned in Jerusalem. I have experienced first-hand what it means to be exceptionally wise and intelligent.”
<verse eid="ECC 1:16"/><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ECC 1:17"/>Having said this, I resolved to learn more about what it means to be wise, as well as what it means to be stupid and insane, by personal experience. I discovered that even these pursuits were as silly as trying to catch the wind.

<verse eid="ECC 1:17"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ECC 1:18"/>It is like people also say:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 1:18">“The wiser one gets, the angrier and more depressed they feel;
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 1:18">knowing more about life only makes life hurt more.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="ECC 1:18"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 1"/>
  <chapter number="2" style="c" sid="ECC 2"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 2:1"/>Then I said to myself, “Let me experiment. I will try to make myself as happy and satisfied as possible by doing everything that I enjoy.” Now pay attention! I concluded that even the joy and satisfaction I experienced from these pleasures were as temporary as the  mist of my breath.
<verse eid="ECC 2:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 2:2"/>So I thought deeply and concluded that giddy laughter is the behavior of crazy people. Likewise, I reasoned that merely pursuing what I enjoyed did me no good.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:2"/><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 2:3"/>So  I thought deeply about the effect that indulging myself with wine or letting myself behave stupidly might have on me. All the while, I remained sober and alert. I thought about this until I could discern through my experiences the best use of people’s short lives on this earth.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:3"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 2:4"/>So I did as many memorable and impressive things as I could. I ordered slaves to build palaces for my leisure and to till and plant vineyards for my pleasure.
<verse eid="ECC 2:4"/><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 2:5"/>I commissioned royal groves of fruit trees in my honor and gardens with flowing streams. Into those groves, I ordered that slaves should plant many kinds of fruit-bearing trees.
<verse eid="ECC 2:5"/><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 2:6"/>Furthermore, I ordered the construction of irrigation pools and canals that provided water for entire forests of lush growth to honor me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:6"/><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 2:7"/>I purchased male and female slaves, and they bore children who were also my slaves in my palace. I also owned more livestock of herd and flock animals than any other previous king in Jerusalem’s history before me.
<verse eid="ECC 2:7"/><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 2:8"/>Moreover, I accumulated large amounts of silver and gold that I obtained from the tributary gifts of kings and the rulers of foreign territories. I owned slaves, both men and women, who sang for me, and I married wives and concubines, who provided me with sexual pleasure.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:8"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 2:9"/>After all my efforts, I had attained more honor, power, and wealth than any king who had ever reigned before me in Jerusalem. Yet I continued to think soberly so that I could evaluate all of my work properly.
<verse eid="ECC 2:9"/><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 2:10"/>In all my efforts, I allowed myself to indulge in every desire that I had. Whatever I wanted, I permitted myself to enjoy it. I did this because my difficult projects made part of me happy, and that experience felt like a reward for all my hard work.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:10"/><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 2:11"/>Then I stopped to think about everything I had accomplished, and all the difficult work that I had done to accomplish it. Pay attention! I concluded that both my effort and everything I achieved were as fleeting and insubstantial as the mist of my breath. My labors had been as silly as trying to catch the wind. Nothing I did provided me with any joy that made life worthwhile.

<verse eid="ECC 2:11"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 2:12"/>This led me to start thinking about the potential benefits of being wise alongside those of being crazy or stupid. I did this because, being king, I had more opportunity to investigate these issues  than any other human being.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:12"/><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 2:13"/>And then I recognized that being wise is far more helpful in life than being stupid, just like it is easier to live well when one has a source of light by which to see than when there is only darkness.

<verse eid="ECC 2:13"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 2:14"/>It is like people say:
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 2:14">A wise person is able to live well,
</para>
  <para style="q3" vid="ECC 2:14">just like someone who walks in the daylight can see.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 2:14">A foolish person, however, endangers themselves with their poor decisions,
</para>
  <para style="q3" vid="ECC 2:14">just like someone who walks about in the dark cannot see.

</para>
  <para style="m" vid="ECC 2:14">Yet even so, I also recognize that the same outcome awaits both wise people and foolish people: Both will die.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:14"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 2:15"/>This made me say to myself, “I am going to die just as inevitably as stupid people! In this way, all my hard work to become a wise person appears to have made no difference! What was the point?” I reminded myself that even my attempts to become wise and live a fulfilled life were as fleeting as the mist of my breath.
<verse eid="ECC 2:15"/><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 2:16"/>After all, no one remembers anyone who dies, whether they lived wisely or stupidly, for very long. After a while, people will forget about both of them. How sad! No matter whether we live wisely or act like an idiot, everyone will eventually forget about us!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:16"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ECC 2:17"/>Because the job of being a human in this life includes enduring these realities that are burdensome, I began to despise being alive. Everything seemed to be as insubstantial as the mist of my breath and as silly as trying to catch the wind.
<verse eid="ECC 2:17"/><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ECC 2:18"/>Then I began to despise all the difficult work that I had labored to do during my life, especially because after I die, someone else will inherit everything that I have  acquired in my life.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:18"/><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ECC 2:19"/>And no one can predict whether my successor will be a wise or a stupid person. Either way, he will have the ability to enjoy all the things that I labored so hard and so wisely for in my life. Regardless, even his enjoyment of the results of my hard work will be as fleeting as the mist of my breath.

<verse eid="ECC 2:19"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ECC 2:20"/>As I thought further about all my life’s work, I began to feel hopeless and depressed.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:20"/><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ECC 2:21"/>Partly I felt this way because it is possible that someone might labor at his work wisely and expertly, even skillfully. But when he dies, his successor will inherit everything, even though he did not do any of the hard work that produced his inheritance! Yet even these painful realities are as fleeting as the mist of my breath.
<verse eid="ECC 2:21"/><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ECC 2:22"/>After all, the emotionally and physically difficult labor that people do throughout their lives does not seem to produce anything that is both good and enduring for them.
<verse eid="ECC 2:22"/><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ECC 2:23"/>This is because every day only seems to cause one pain. Furthermore, the work people do only produces grief. It is so grievous that, when they ought to be asleep, people’s minds are so anxious that they cannot rest. Yet even so, all of this is as insubstantial as the mist.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:23"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ECC 2:24"/>So, the best way that people can live is  to enjoy whatever they have to eat and drink and to allow themselves to find something enjoyable about their work. I have personally experienced that this  joy only comes when God gives it to someone.
<verse eid="ECC 2:24"/><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ECC 2:25"/>After all, there is not a single human being who can enjoy the things he has if God does not first give those things to him.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 2:25"/><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ECC 2:26"/>God does this by making the people of whom he approves wise, intelligent, and happy. Yet God has decided that whoever disobeys him will work hard only so that they gain wealth that God will give to those of whom he approves. Even their wealth and hard work are as fleeting as the mist of my breath, and as silly as trying to catch the wind.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="ECC 2:26"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 2"/>
  <chapter number="3" style="c" sid="ECC 3"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 3:1"/>There is a fixed moment at which everything in this life happens, and a determined period for every kind of event that happens during a person’s life:
<verse eid="ECC 3:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 3:2"/>People will give birth, and people will die,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:2">people will plant their crops, and people will reap and harvest those crops.
<verse eid="ECC 3:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 3:3"/>People will kill people and people will heal people,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:3">people will tear structures down, and people will build structures.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 3:4"/>People will cry and people will laugh,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:4">people will grieve and people will celebrate.
<verse eid="ECC 3:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 3:5"/>People will throw rocks, and people will gather rocks;
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:5">people will hug, and people will refuse to hug.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 3:6"/>People will look for lost things, and people will lose found things;
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:6">people will store things, and people will throw things away.
<verse eid="ECC 3:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 3:7"/>People will rip their clothing {in grief}, and people will sew clothing back together;
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:7">people will stay quiet, and people will talk.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 3:8"/>People will love one another, and people will hate one another;
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:8">people will go to war, and people will mediate peace.

<verse eid="ECC 3:8"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 3:9"/>Again, I want to know whether the  difficult labor of human life ever produces any satisfaction or joy  for hard-working people.

<verse eid="ECC 3:9"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 3:10"/>I have observed the job of living in which God has employed every human being to bustle about doing.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:10"/><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 3:11"/>God makes everything that happens  beautifully fitted for the moment that God has determined. And at the same time, God enables humanity to perceive that he is at work in their lives. Even so, God does not allow humanity to  understand everything that God does—from the time that he created this world  to the point at which he brings all things to an end.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:11"/><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 3:12"/>In light of this, I confess that the best way for humanity to live is to gratefully celebrate life and to live ethically while one is alive.
<verse eid="ECC 3:12"/><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 3:13"/>And, furthermore, whenever a person is able to enjoy what they have to eat and drink, and to find something gratifying in the work that they do, this is a priceless thing that God has enabled.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:13"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 3:14"/>I also confess that all of God’s decisions are unchanging. It is like people say:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:14">No one can improve what God does,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 3:14">either by addition or subtraction.

</para>
  <para style="m" vid="ECC 3:14">God does this so that they submit  to God  in trusting faith.

<verse eid="ECC 3:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 3:15"/>Current events are not new.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 3:15">Things like them have already happened.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 3:15">Likewise, future events will not be new.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 3:15">Things like them are already happening now.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 3:15">God is the one who understands what every person is trying to understand.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:15"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 3:16"/>However, here is something else that I observed: The places in society from which people expect rulers to make just and fair decisions are frequently places in which rulers make unjust and oppressive decisions!
<verse eid="ECC 3:16"/><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ECC 3:17"/>I confessed to myself, “God will hold accountable people who make just decisions and those who make unjust decisions. He will do this because there is a moment for everything that happens, including God’s judgment of rulers in those places.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:17"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ECC 3:18"/>This led me to confess further to myself: “God is governing the world this way because he wants people to clearly understand that they are really no different than animals.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:18"/><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ECC 3:19"/>After all, human lives and animal lives end in the same way. People die just like animals die, and they seem to possess the same capacity to die. Because of this, it appears that being human offers no benefit over being an animal. All life is as fleeting as the mist of my breath.
<verse eid="ECC 3:19"/><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ECC 3:20"/>When people and animals die, people bury both in the ground. Both people and animals are as mortal as if God made them from dirt, and when each dies their dead bodies become dirt once again.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 3:20"/><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ECC 3:21"/>This causes me to wonder whether people are correct about what happens to people when they die. Perhaps humans ascend to the place in which God lives, and animals descend into  the ground.

<verse eid="ECC 3:21"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ECC 3:22"/>So, I confess once again that the best way for people to live is to allow themselves to enjoy what their work produces—even the hard work itself. This is because God has given each person their work. It is their daily allotment. After all, nobody can know what will happen tomorrow.


<verse eid="ECC 3:22"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 3"/>
  <chapter number="4" style="c" sid="ECC 4"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 4:1"/>Next, as I continued to contemplate, I observed all the ways in which people suffer from oppression during their lives. Now pay attention! As I watched those people cry, I realized that no one was there to reassure them in their grief. Furthermore, those who oppressed them appeared to possess power such that no one was able to reassure them in their grief.
<verse eid="ECC 4:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 4:2"/>This sad reality made me feel like those people who have already died are much more fortunate than the people who remain alive.
<verse eid="ECC 4:2"/><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 4:3"/>Yet the people whose mothers have not yet given birth to them are even more fortunate than both people currently alive and those people who have already died. Indeed, the unborn have not yet had to experience or witness any of the evil  actions that humans do during their lives.

<verse eid="ECC 4:3"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 4:4"/>Then I observed that people work as hard as they do and strive to be successful only because they are jealous of their friends’ possessions. Yet even their ill-motivated labor is as insubstantial as the mist of my breath and as futile as trying to catch the wind.

<verse eid="ECC 4:4"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 4:5"/>It is just like people say:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 4:5">Rather than work hard, foolish people merely relax
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:5">with their unproductive hands folded on their laps, ready to eat their meal.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 4:5">Yet as they do so, they unwittingly ruin themselves,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:5">as if they were feasting on their own bodies!

<verse eid="ECC 4:5"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 4:6"/>People also say this:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 4:6">“Despite appearances, being contented with a simple, peaceful lifestyle is twice as good
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:6">as being rich but living a life of stressful labor—as frustrating as trying to catch the wind.”

<verse eid="ECC 4:6"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 4:7"/>I proceeded to contemplate another facet of people’s lives—one that also seemed as insubstantial as the mist.
<verse eid="ECC 4:7"/><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 4:8"/>I saw someone who lived alone. He appeared to have no children nor any brothers or sisters living with him. And every day, the person labored without stopping. Even though he was wealthy, he never felt satisfied with his life and possessions. Then he asked himself, “Why am I allowing my hard work to deprive me of things that I enjoy?” His attempts to satisfy himself with a lavish lifestyle were as insubstantial as the mist. His joyless labor was really the most lamentable part of the difficult job of being alive in which God has employed humanity.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 4:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 4:9"/>Having a friend is more advantageous than living one’s life alone.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:9">After all, together  two people can experience better outcomes in their hard work.
<verse eid="ECC 4:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 4:10"/>First, if you were to fall down, a friend could help you get up again,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:10">but if you are alone and you fall down,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:10">you will only experience hardship and pain,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:10">because no one will be there to help you stand up again.
<verse eid="ECC 4:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 4:11"/>Second, if you and a friend were to lie down to sleep in the cold,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:11">you could help one another stay warm,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:11">but if you try to sleep in the cold alone,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:11">of course you will be cold!
<verse eid="ECC 4:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 4:12"/>Third, if someone stronger than you were to attack you,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:12">he might beat you up by yourself.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:12">However you and your friend would be able to ward off your attacker together!
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 4:12">Three people could defend themselves even more easily,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:12">just as a rope that someone made from three strands
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 4:12">is harder to rip in half than a rope with one or two strands.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 4:12"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 4:13"/>It is preferable to be a poor young man who nonetheless acts wisely than to be a king who, despite his age, acts like a fool and refuses to pay attention to good advice, as he did when he was younger.
<verse eid="ECC 4:13"/><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 4:14"/>I imagine that it is possible for a young man like that to live so successfully that he becomes king—even ruling over the land in which his mother gave birth to him in poverty, so severe that it was like someone tying her up with ropes.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 4:14"/><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 4:15"/>But then I imagine all of the people in this young man’s kingdom who merely go about their lives. The person who will be the successor to the young king is among them.
<verse eid="ECC 4:15"/><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 4:16"/>Despite his current popularity with the endless crowds of his subjects, after a few years future generations will be tired of him. So even the wise way he lived that led to his success  is as fleeting as the mist of my breath and as frustrating as trying to catch the wind.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="ECC 4:16"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 4"/>
  <chapter number="5" style="c" sid="ECC 5"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 5:1"/>Be mindful of how you behave when you go to worship God at his house. Draw near to him  by listening expectantly. That is far better than offering sacrifices to God in the ways that foolish people do. They are ignorant that they are behaving inappropriately in God’s presence!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 5:2"/>Do not be so quick to say something  that you speak rashly. Neither  should you allow  yourself  to raise  an issue  hastily  in God’s presence. You ought to remember that  this God  is the God who rules from heaven  and that you  live here on the dust of the earth. For this reason, you ought to think before you say anything in God’s presence.

<verse eid="ECC 5:2"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 5:3"/>After all, it is like people say:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 5:3">“When someone claims to receive secret messages from the gods,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:3">he tends to rush busily about his life.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 5:3">And whenever foolish people talk,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:3">they tend to speak in as many words as possible.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:3"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 5:4"/>If you solemnly promise God that you will offer a sacrifice to him, you ought to do what you promised quickly. Neither God nor his people tolerate foolish people. You should be careful to do whatever you promise.
<verse eid="ECC 5:4"/><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 5:5"/>It is better to not promise anything to God than to promise to do something and then fail to do it!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:5"/><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 5:6"/>You should never talk in such a way that you begin to sin. It would be shameful if you promised God that you would offer him a sacrifice only to lie and claim that you made a mistake when God’s priest arrived to collect the sacrificial animal. If you were to do that, God would be so frustrated with your so-called promises that he would destroy everything you have worked to accomplish!

<verse eid="ECC 5:6"/><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 5:7"/>This is the point: While everyone else is chasing messages from the gods, offering the prolific prayers of foolish people, or doing whatever vain things people do in God’s presence, you ought to live your life fearfully remembering that God is God!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:7"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 5:8"/>You may witness powerful people where you live extorting money from impoverished people or corrupting legal systems to their own benefit. You should not be surprised about this. Lamentably, this is the way things are. The people who can hold these powerful people accountable watch and do nothing. And even these more powerful people are under the authority of someone even higher who likewise does nothing.
<verse eid="ECC 5:8"/><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 5:9"/>Also, all of these corrupt officials seize the harvests of our fields—even the king enjoys the food that his subjects collect from our farms.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 5:10"/>Some people believe that only wealth can provide them
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:10">with the kind of happy life that they desire.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 5:10">These people will never think that they have enough money,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:10">nor will any number of possessions  make them  happy.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 5:10">In reality, money is also as  insubstantial
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:10">as the  mist of my breath.
<verse eid="ECC 5:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 5:11"/>The more wealth that an individual has,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:11">the more people seek to benefit from that individual’s wealth.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 5:11">So rich people do not really benefit from their wealth.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:11">It only seems to others that their wealth helps them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 5:12"/>Poor hardworking people sleep soundly at night
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:12">no matter whether they have a lot of food or only a little to eat.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 5:12">Like a person who sleeps poorly because he has eaten too much,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:12">the wealth that rich people believe provides them with a satisfying  life
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 5:12">actually makes them so anxious that they are unable to fall asleep.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:12"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 5:13"/>I have observed that money is the cause of another lamentable, gut-wrenching situation that can happen to people during their lives. I have watched someone save all of his substantial income. But  his wealth ultimately caused him  pain.
<verse eid="ECC 5:13"/><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 5:14"/>When his investments unexpectedly failed, he lost his entire savings. He had planned to give this wealth to his son as an inheritance. Yet now he had nothing left to pass on.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:14"/><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 5:15"/>And so  that man died in the same condition as when his mother gave birth to him: He began his life naked, possessing no wealth, and so he died in the same way. When he died, that man’s  labor had never produced for him any meaningful sense of satisfaction.

<verse eid="ECC 5:15"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 5:16"/>Yes, this was the lamentable, gut-wrenching situation that I witnessed: This man brought nothing into the world when his life began, and when he died, he took nothing with him. And so, it seems that his hard work never enabled him to feel satisfied. His work was like a frustrated attempt to grab a handful of the wind.
<verse eid="ECC 5:16"/><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ECC 5:17"/>Moreover, every day of his life he sat in the dark when he ate his meals—deeply depressed, fatigued from illness, and fuming.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:17"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ECC 5:18"/>Now pay attention! I have observed how beautiful life can be when people merely enjoy the food and drink that they have and allow themselves to feel some satisfying happiness in their hard work. People can do this only while they are alive during the brief span of life that God has allotted to them as his gift—no more and no less.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 5:18"/><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ECC 5:19"/>God sometimes gives people money and possessions. But only sometimes does he also give them the ability to enjoy what they have. When God does, people accept what God has given them—no more and no less—and are able to enjoy their otherwise difficult and stressful work. This joyful acceptance is something priceless, like a gift from God.
<verse eid="ECC 5:19"/><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ECC 5:20"/>By enabling us to accept the good things in our lives joyfully, God focuses our attention on what we enjoy about our lives. This allows us to forget, momentarily, the reality that one day we will inevitably die.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="ECC 5:20"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 5"/>
  <chapter number="6" style="c" sid="ECC 6"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 6:1"/>Sadly, I have also observed the painful realities that happen to people during their lives. This lamentable part of life is deeply distressing and difficult to accept.
<verse eid="ECC 6:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 6:2"/>God sometimes makes people so  wealthy and well-respected that they have everything that they could want. Yet sometimes God does not allow these people to enjoy those  things that their wealth provides. Instead, someone outside their family acquires their wealth and enjoys it. Yet even still, money remains as fleeting as the mist of my breath. It is gut-wrenching and lamentable that this can happen.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 6:2"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 6:3"/>Someone could perhaps father very many children and live a very long life. No matter how long his life might be—even if he were to go on living forever—if he never allows himself to enjoy the wonderful parts of his life, I will consider the life of a baby who dies when it is still in its mother’s womb to be more desirable than this man’s miserable life.
<verse eid="ECC 6:3"/><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 6:4"/>True, this baby’s life was as brief as the mist, and it died in its mother’s womb, never having seen the light. The baby’s parents were never able to name their child, so that the child’s life will be only a sad memory.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 6:4"/><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 6:5"/>The stillborn baby did not even live to experience life under the light of the sun. Yet neither did it have to feel the burden of a difficult life. So, despite its brief life, the stillborn baby found more peace  than that miserable man.
<verse eid="ECC 6:5"/><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 6:6"/>His life would not improve even if that man were to go on living for two thousand years. He, like every human being, only ever endured the painful parts of life, but he never enjoyed the good parts of his life. Truthfully, the man’s life will come to an end just as the stillborn baby’s will, and both of them experience death—just like every human being.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 6:6"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 6:7"/>It is like people say:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 6:7">“Everyone works hard to satiate their appetites,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 6:7">but no one ever  satisfies their deepest  cravings.”

<verse eid="ECC 6:7"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 6:8"/>Consider this: Living wisely does not seem any more able than living foolishly to keep a person from suffering and  dying. In the same way, knowing how to conduct oneself wisely  does not seem to  lengthen or improve the life of someone who is poor.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 6:8"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 6:9"/>It is like people also say:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 6:9">“It is better to enjoy the things that one already has
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 6:9">than to allow one’s impulsive desire to inform one’s decisions.”

</para>
  <para style="m" vid="ECC 6:9">These realities are as insubstantial as the mist, and as futile as trying to catch the wind.

<verse eid="ECC 6:9"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 6:10"/>Before anything happens, God has already declared what it should be. It is evident to everyone that human beings are merely mortal. So,  it is pointless to complain to God about the parts of life that we dislike. Humans are not all-powerful or all-knowing like God is!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 6:10"/><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 6:11"/>It sometimes seems as if our complaints about life only produce more words that are as insubstantial as the mist. This does not appear to benefit anyone.
<verse eid="ECC 6:11"/><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 6:12"/>Certainly, I wonder whether anyone can  know what he ought to do during his limited lifetime. People live for a short time, and their lives  seem as fleeting as the mist. Indeed, God has designed human life so that it lasts  briefly, just like a passing shadow. Moreover, God seems to want human beings to be ignorant about what might happen to them tomorrow.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="ECC 6:12"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 6"/>
  <chapter number="7" style="c" sid="ECC 7"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 7:1"/>An honorable reputation is more advantageous
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:1">than expensive perfume.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:1">Similarly, a person’s death is a more advantageous moment
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:1">than the moment when that person’s mother gave birth to him.
<verse eid="ECC 7:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 7:2"/>Attending a funeral is more useful
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:2">than attending a celebratory feast.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:2">This is true because everyone will die.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:2">People should take time to ponder this reality while they are alive.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 7:3"/>Feeling grief and mourning is more advantageous
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:3">than laughing.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:3">This is true because grieving painful experiences may help one
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:3">better understand what life is like and accept it.
<verse eid="ECC 7:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 7:4"/>Wise people participate in public ceremonies of mourning
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:4">so that they may ponder the fact that they also will  die.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:4">Conversely, foolish people ignore this reality
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:4">by only attending celebrations and festive parties.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 7:5"/>Listening to the criticism of someone who lives wisely is more advantageous
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:5">than listening to the song of foolish people.
<verse eid="ECC 7:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 7:6"/>This is true because the laughter of foolish people
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:6">is as useless as trying to heat food
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:6">over a fire that someone fuels with thorns.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:6">Although the thorns may loudly pop,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:6">they will only produce a little heat.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:6">In the same way, the loud noise of foolish people
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:6">is as insubstantial as the mist.

<verse eid="ECC 7:6"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 7:7"/>Now, the power to abuse someone less powerful 
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:7">can turn even wise people into  foolish people.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:7">In the same way, officials who accept dishonest payment 
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:7">become unable to do what is right.

<verse eid="ECC 7:7"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 7:8"/>It is better for people for something to conclude
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:8">than for it merely to begin.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:8">Similarly, being patient is  more advantageous
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:8">than being proud.
<verse eid="ECC 7:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 7:9"/>You should not allow yourself to lose your temper quickly,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:9">because being irritable is characteristic of foolish people.

<verse eid="ECC 7:9"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 7:10"/>Neither should you complain and wonder why it seems that life in the past was preferable to the present.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:10">Surely only foolish nostalgia, not wise reflection, causes this kind of comment.

<verse eid="ECC 7:10"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 7:11"/>Thinking and living wisely is both valuable and useful,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:11">just like receiving an endowment of property or money from a relative who has died.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:11">As such, being wise can provide anyone alive with significant benefits.
<verse eid="ECC 7:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 7:12"/>Their ability to think and act wisely can provide protection for  people,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:12">just like money can sometimes provide protection for wealthy people.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:12">Nonetheless, knowing how to think and live well has one more benefit than being wealthy:
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:12">This kind of wise thinking often prevents people
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:12">from doing foolish things that would cause  undesirable outcomes.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:12"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 7:13"/>Think carefully about what God has done. Certainly, no human being can change those aspects of their lives that they dislike, since God has determined that their lives should be that way.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 7:14"/>When your life is going well for you, enjoy it!
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:14">But when your life is not going the way that you would prefer, stop to consider this:
</para>
  <para style="q3" vid="ECC 7:14">God is the one who created life and causes
</para>
  <para style="q4" vid="ECC 7:14">both good things and less desirable things to happen.
</para>
  <para style="q3" vid="ECC 7:14">God does this so that we do not start thinking
</para>
  <para style="q4" vid="ECC 7:14">that we can control what happens to us.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:14"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 7:15"/>In my lifetime, as fleeting as the fading mist of my breath, I have witnessed two opposite scenarios.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:15">On the one hand, many good people die  young, even though they live justly.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:15">On the other hand, many bad people live long and prosperous lives, even though they live unjustly.
<verse eid="ECC 7:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 7:16"/>So, you should not think that you can be so virtuous
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:16">that your  virtue obligates God to give you a long and prosperous life.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:16">Neither should you think that you can become so wise 
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:16">that you can avoid anything painful in life.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:16">If you were to think this way, you would only be astonished  when undesirable things happen.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ECC 7:17"/>You should not live so sinfully
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:17">that God becomes angry at you,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:17">and neither should you allow yourself to live foolishly.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:17">If you were to live this way, your foolishness could cause you to die young.
<verse eid="ECC 7:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ECC 7:18"/>Your life will go best if you are diligent in remembering to live according to
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:18">both parts of my advice to you.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:18">The kind of individual who recognizes that God is governing this world and submits to him will demonstrate this by living as both parts of my advice have instructed him.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:18"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ECC 7:19"/>The ability to think and live wisely can guide an individual safely through his life. It can do this more effectively than even the ten most powerful officials who govern a city!
<verse eid="ECC 7:19"/><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ECC 7:20"/>Humanity needs the protection that comes from thinking and living wisely, because no one who has ever lived has been entirely righteous. No human being has ever done only what is good and right without ever doing anything wrong.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:20"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ECC 7:21"/>Relatedly, you should not pay attention to everything that people say. If you were to do that, you might inadvertently hear people whom you trust publicly demean you.
<verse eid="ECC 7:21"/><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ECC 7:22"/>After all, you yourself know very well that you have also publicly demeaned people who trusted you.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:22"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ECC 7:23"/>I used my ability to think and live wisely to analyze all of these realities of life. I resolved that I would become wise enough to understand all the realities that I have witnessed in my life. Frustratingly, I could not do it.
<verse eid="ECC 7:23"/><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ECC 7:24"/>All the present realities that I observed were beyond my comprehension, as if they were too far away for me to see clearly. They were also  difficult for me to understand, as if they were too deep for me to get to the bottom of them. I do not believe that any human being can  understand all of this.
<verse eid="ECC 7:24"/><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ECC 7:25"/>Nevertheless, I shifted my attention to a larger task. I wanted to study humanity’s ability to think and live wisely so that I could comprehend the way that God governs everything and the reason that he governs the way he does. I also wanted to study humanity’s tendency to live so foolishly that they openly disobey God and to live so irrationally that they become fools.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:25"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ECC 7:26"/>This is what I learned:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:26">Foolish thinking is as painful as one’s death.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:26">It is like a seductive woman
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:26">who attempts to ensnare young men.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:26">Her desire is to trap and deceive people,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:26">as if her very heart were a cage,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:26">and her very hands were iron chains.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:26">Foolish thinking is like such a woman.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:26">Although foolish thinking like that will capture sinful people,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:26">those who live well in God’s presence will be able to avoid foolish living.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:26"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ECC 7:27"/>So, understand—this is what I, Qoheleth, have learned from my investigations:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:27">I carefully considered one thing after another until I could comprehend the way that God governs everything and the reason that he governs the way he does.
<verse eid="ECC 7:27"/></para>
  <para style="q2"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="ECC 7:28"/>Despite all my failures, I continued searching,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:28">but I could not find what I was desperate to discover.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:28">I was looking to become wise,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:28">as if the ability to think and live wisely was an elusive woman living among a thousand men.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:28">I found all the men, but I never found that woman.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 7:28"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="ECC 7:29"/>So, consider this:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:29">After everything, I have learned only that when God created humanity,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:29">God made them to live justly and equitably.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 7:29">Despite this, humans have tried to discover innumerable ways
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 7:29">to understand life and attempt to control what happens to them.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="ECC 7:29"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 7"/>
  <chapter number="8" style="c" sid="ECC 8"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 8:1"/>Wise people are truly unparalleled!
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:1">They have the  ability to solve difficult problems.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 8:1">Being wise can make people visibly happy
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:1">and transform the  grim look of their faces.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 8:1"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 8:2"/>Now, here is my counsel: God supports the king who represents God and rules with his authority. Because of this divine support, you should pay attention to and comply with the king’s directives.
<verse eid="ECC 8:2"/><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 8:3"/>Do not let this king intimidate you. If the king should ever order you to do something immoral or unjust, you should not comply. Leave immediately! After all, the king has the power to do whatever he wants—including commanding someone to kill you!
<verse eid="ECC 8:3"/><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 8:4"/>This is because the king possesses absolute authority, such that none of his subjects could disobey him without risking harm to himself. No one would ever dare to question the king’s actions or demand an explanation for what he commands.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 8:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 8:5"/>The king will not harm anyone
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:5">who pays attention to what the king commands
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:5">and complies with his directives.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 8:5">In light of this, someone who is wise will need to discern
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:5">how to respond to the king’s authority with careful timing
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:5">and appropriate behavior.
<verse eid="ECC 8:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 8:6"/>They will need to know this because,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:6">although human beings endure all sorts of lamentable realities in life,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 8:6">in every situation in which people find themselves,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:6">there is always a proper way to do something
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:6">and an appropriate moment at which to do it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 8:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 8:7"/>One of the most lamentable of these realities is that the events of the future are hidden to human beings.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:7">After all, no human can foresee the future well enough to tell others about it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 8:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 8:8"/>God has not enabled anyone to control the wind;
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 8:8">in a similar way, no one can control when they will die,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:8">just like soldiers cannot decide to go home while they are fighting a battle.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 8:8">This is true even for those people who disobey God.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:8">Their attempts to control their own lives apart from God
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:8">cannot give them mastery over what is uncontrollable.

<verse eid="ECC 8:8"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 8:9"/>As I considered the kinds of things that happen to people during their lives, I witnessed all these lamentable realities happening. I saw that sometimes people like the king possess such power over other human beings that they dominate and harm them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 8:9"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 8:10"/>Moreover, I observed the honor that people gave to wicked people at their funerals. I had daily witnessed these wicked people going in and out of the sacred places where people worship God, and people in the city praised them for their behavior! Yet even this is only temporary, as fleeting as a  mist.
<verse eid="ECC 8:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 8:11"/>First, people in power sometimes administer justice against evil actions too slowly.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:11">For this reason, many people feel encouraged to plan
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:11">and commit evil actions without fear of negative consequences.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 8:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 8:12"/>Second, sometimes sinful people commit a hundred crimes, and yet they live a  long life.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 8:12">Despite this, I am confident that the lives of people who understand that God is powerful and just and good—those who fearfully submit themselves to God—will turn out well in the end.
<verse eid="ECC 8:12"/></para>
  <para style="q2"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 8:13"/>I am also confident of the opposite: The lives of people who disobey God will not go well. Just like shadows in the setting sun appear to stretch on forever—in the same way, the current prosperity of evil people is an illusion. After all, they feel no fear when they think of God’s power and justice!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 8:13"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 8:14"/>It is only a temporary reality of this life that the undesirable things that bad people deserve sometimes happen to good people, and the kind of lives that good people deserve seem to happen to bad people. I confess that, despite current appearances, these realities are also as temporary as a  mist.

<verse eid="ECC 8:14"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 8:15"/>Living life joyfully is worth celebrating! The best that anyone can do during one’s life is simply to eat and drink and be happy. For as long as this God graciously decides a person should live, this kind of simple joy will remain with him like a faithful companion, no matter what difficult labor he must do.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 8:15"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 8:16"/>I then resolved within myself to become as wise as I could. I wanted to observe the difficult parts of people’s lives so that I could understand them, even though they can make people so anxious that they cannot sleep at night or during the day.
<verse eid="ECC 8:16"/><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ECC 8:17"/>When I did this, I thought about everything that God has done. Then I realized that he has made humanity so that they can never fully comprehend everything that God does in their lives. Although people will never stop trying to understand, they will never succeed. Even if wise people claim that they understand it all, they really cannot.


<verse eid="ECC 8:17"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 8"/>
  <chapter number="9" style="c" sid="ECC 9"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 9:1"/>Truly, I thought deeply about all these realities so that I could study them, including this one: It is God who controls what happens to everyone and what the labor that they do produces. This is true for wise people and those who live justly. However, people never know what will happen to them in the future. Even for  righteous people, both undesirable and enjoyable things may happen to them.
<verse eid="ECC 9:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 9:2"/>Everyone dies eventually, no matter who they are—
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:2">whether they lived righteously or wickedly,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:2">whether they lived well and were ceremonially clean and able to worship God, or they were ceremonially unclean and unable to worship God,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:2">whether they offered sacrifices to God or refrained from offering sacrifices.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:2">Good people and sinful people will both  die,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:2">whether they did what they promised God that they would do, or they were afraid to make such promises.

<verse eid="ECC 9:2"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 9:3"/>Yes, this is a lamentable part of being alive on this earth. Eventually everyone dies. But even more so, during the time that they are alive, it seems that everyone is fully intent on living like evil people. They also live like crazy people! And then after that, they die just like everyone else!

<verse eid="ECC 9:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 9:4"/>Yet as long as someone is alive,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:4">one will have the opportunity to enjoy good things.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:4">Because of this, it is better to be alive than to be dead.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:4">Even though people think little of dogs in comparison to lions,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:4">the dog that is alive is better off than the lion that is dead.
<verse eid="ECC 9:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 9:5"/>This is because people who are still alive are  aware that they will die someday,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:5">but dead people know nothing!
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:5">Also, dead people have lost their opportunity to enjoy the good things that people can experience during their lives.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:5">Furthermore, people will  forget about them!

<verse eid="ECC 9:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 9:6"/>While they are alive, people love other people.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:6">They hate people, and they envy people.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:6">However, the things that these people felt died long ago.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:6">Those who have died will never again have the chance
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:6">to enjoy anything good that happens during people’s lives.

<verse eid="ECC 9:6"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 9:7"/>So, go and live your life! Allow yourself to be happy when you eat your food and to enjoy the experience of drinking wine. Remember: God wants you to do this!
<verse eid="ECC 9:7"/><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 9:8"/>Wear your best clothing all the time, and keep yourself groomed as if you were going to a party!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 9:8"/><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 9:9"/>Enjoy the experience of living your fleeting life with your beloved wife. God has given you whatever  time you continue to breathe, though it is as brief as the  mist of my breath. God has apportioned this time for you to enjoy with your wife, even while you labor at your  work.
<verse eid="ECC 9:9"/><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 9:10"/>Whatever you can do, do it with all your energy. You should do this because you will die eventually. When you die and your family buries you in the ground, you will no longer be able to accomplish anything  or understand why life happens the way it does—or know or understand anything!

<verse eid="ECC 9:10"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 9:11"/>Once again, I witnessed this reality about life:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:11">The person who runs fastest does not always win the race,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:11">nor do the strongest soldiers always win the battle,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:11">nor do the wisest people always have enough food to eat,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:11">nor do the most insightful people always become financially prosperous,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:11">nor are the people who know the most always the most successful.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:11">No human being can control or predict what will happen to them.

<verse eid="ECC 9:11"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 9:12"/>Even more so, no one can predict when he will die.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:12">People catch fish in deadly nets,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:12">and people catch birds in traps.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:12">Similarly, every human being will experience their inevitable death
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:12">just like those fish and birds,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:12">like a deadly trap that seizes them unexpectedly.

<verse eid="ECC 9:12"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 9:13"/>I also observed this truth about wisdom in this life, and it made a lasting  impact on me:
<verse eid="ECC 9:13"/><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 9:14"/>There was once a small town, and only a few people lived there. Then a powerful  king and his army attacked that town and besieged it on all sides. The army built massive ramps against the walls so that they could climb up and conquer the town.
<verse eid="ECC 9:14"/><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 9:15"/>Now in that town, there was a man who was poor but wise nonetheless. Remarkably, the man thought wisely and counseled actions that rescued the town from the attack. However, in a little while nobody remembered the poor man who rescued them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 9:15"/><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 9:16"/>So I concluded that it is more beneficial for people to be wise than to be strong. Nonetheless, it is also true that no one values the wise thinking of poor people, nor will they listen to what they say.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 9:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ECC 9:17"/>People will  listen to a wise person who speaks softly more attentively
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:17">than they will to a king who  shouts at a crowd of foolish people.
<verse eid="ECC 9:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ECC 9:18"/>Being wise is actually a more effective  instrument
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:18">than the weapons that soldiers use to fight wars.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 9:18">Even so, the  actions of a single sinful person have the potential to ruin
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 9:18">the good things that many other people have accomplished.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="ECC 9:18"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 9"/>
  <chapter number="10" style="c" sid="ECC 10"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 10:1"/>If only a few flies die in a vat of perfume,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:1">the entire batch can become rancid and stinking.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:1">Similarly, one foolish decision can have a disastrous effect
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:1">on a lifetime of wise and honorable decisions.
<verse eid="ECC 10:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 10:2"/>A person’s wise thinking can protect them,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:2">like the right hand of a warrior that grips his sword.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:2">Yet people who think foolishly are vulnerable
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:2">to the outcomes of their own poor decision-making.
<verse eid="ECC 10:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 10:3"/>Whenever foolish people travel on  roads,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:3">they lack any common sense.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:3">They themselves make their stupidity obvious
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:3">to everyone who sees them.

<verse eid="ECC 10:3"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 10:4"/>Should someone powerful become enraged with you,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:4">do not abandon your responsibilities with him.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:4">Remember! Calm and composed responses can lessen
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:4">another’s fury at serious offenses.

<verse eid="ECC 10:4"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 10:5"/>I have  observed another lamentable situation in my life:
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:5">Sometimes people in authority unintentionally make public mistakes.
<verse eid="ECC 10:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 10:6"/>They  promote many foolish people to  positions of authority,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:6">while  they assign wealthy people to unimportant positions.
<verse eid="ECC 10:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 10:7"/>I have witnessed people promote slaves so that they ride on horses
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:7">like rich people usually do.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:7">And yet, they force important officials to walk in the dirt
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:7">like slaves usually do.

<verse eid="ECC 10:7"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 10:8"/>If someone digs a hole in the ground, he might fall into it.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:8">If someone tears down a wall, a snake living inside the wall might bite him.
<verse eid="ECC 10:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 10:9"/>If someone quarries rocks, the loosened rocks might fall and injure him.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:9">If someone chops wood, the split logs might wound him.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 10:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 10:10"/>If a person’s axe head is dull,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:10">and he has not sharpened its edges,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:10">then in order to chop something,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:10">he will have to exert more force.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:10">This all shows that being wise is the secret
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:10">to success in using one’s tools.
<verse eid="ECC 10:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 10:11"/>If a snake bites someone before he can calm it,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:11">then an ability to calm snakes is of no use to him.

<verse eid="ECC 10:11"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 10:12"/>People will  honor wise people
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:12">because of the  way in which they speak.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:12">However, the things that foolish people say
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:12">will inevitably harm them,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:12">as if their own lips were to swallow them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 10:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 10:13"/>When foolish people begin to talk,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:13">they  say stupid things,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:13">but by the time they are finished,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:13">they have said things that are madness and exceedingly harmful.
<verse eid="ECC 10:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 10:14"/>Yet the foolish person will continue talking.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:14">Nobody knows what might happen in the future.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:14">After all, there is no human being who can tell someone
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:14">about what will happen to them tomorrow!

<verse eid="ECC 10:14"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ECC 10:15"/>Foolish people become very tired by the work that they do,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:15">with the result that they are even unable to find the road to their town.

<verse eid="ECC 10:15"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ECC 10:16"/>It is terrible for the people of a nation
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:16">when their ruler acts like a boy,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:16">and when their leaders use their power
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:16">to consume large meals at inappropriate times.
<verse eid="ECC 10:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ECC 10:17"/>But it is wonderful for a nation
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:17">when its ruler comes from a worthy family,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:17">and when its leaders host large dinners
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:17">only at the appropriate moments—
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:17">eating and drinking in a dignified manner and not in drunken revelry.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 10:17"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ECC 10:18"/>It is like people say:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:18">“Lazy homeowners will neglect their roofs
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:18">to the point that they sag and collapse;
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:18">the idle lifestyle of house owners
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:18">will  cause rainwater to leak into the house.”
<verse eid="ECC 10:18"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ECC 10:19"/>Eating food can make people so happy that they laugh,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:19">and drinking wine can make one’s life joyful.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:19">However, people who have wealth can respond to all of their problems.

<verse eid="ECC 10:19"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ECC 10:20"/>So, you should never dishonor the king.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:20">Do not even think about it!
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:20">Neither should you speak ill of wealthy people,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:20">even when you are alone in your bedroom.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 10:20">After all  it is  possible that a little winged bird
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 10:20">will overhear what you are saying and tell everyone what you said.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="ECC 10:20"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 10"/>
  <chapter number="11" style="c" sid="ECC 11"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 11:1"/>Be generous with what you have,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:1">because eventually others will also be generous to you.
<verse eid="ECC 11:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 11:2"/>Give a share {of your goods} to seven or eight {people},
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 11:2">because in this world you never know
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:2">what bad things might happen {to you}.

<verse eid="ECC 11:2"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 11:3"/>It will rain only when the clouds are ready to do so.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:3">Similarly, a fallen tree will remain
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:3">in the place where it happened to topple over—
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:3">wherever that might be.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 11:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 11:4"/>If someone watches for changes in the wind,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:4">waiting for the perfect moment to plant,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:4">they will never plant their crops.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 11:4">Likewise, if someone watches the clouds,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:4">waiting for the perfect moment to harvest,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:4">they will never harvest their crops.

<verse eid="ECC 11:4"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 11:5"/>Now no one understands where the wind comes from or where it goes, nor does anyone understand how a life forms in a woman’s womb. In this way, human beings cannot fully understand what God is doing in our lives. After all, God is in charge of everything that happens.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 11:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 11:6"/>Plant your crops while it is still morning,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:6">and do not stop planting until you are done—
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:6">even through the evening,
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 11:6">because you do not know which crops will grow better.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:6">One batch of crops might grow better than the other,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:6">or perhaps all of your crops will grow well.

<verse eid="ECC 11:6"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 11:7"/>It is a wonderful thing to be alive,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:7">just like the warm light of the sun is good for people.
<verse eid="ECC 11:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 11:8"/>So should anyone live for many years,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:8">that person should enjoy each one of them.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 11:8">However he should not allow himself to forget
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:8">either the difficult moments in the past
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:8">or that he will grow old and die.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 11:8">He will surely have many moments
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 11:8">as difficult as the dark of night.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 11:8">However they will be as temporary as the mist.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 11:8"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 11:9"/>So, you, young man—let yourself be happy while you are  young. Allow yourself to enjoy being alive before you grow old. Pursue the things that you love to do but never forget that our God will judge you one day. He will discern whether you have enjoyed well your time or squandered it.
<verse eid="ECC 11:9"/><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 11:10"/>Moreover, do not allow yourself to feel angry about life, and do not pay attention to the pains  in your body. Remember: Being a young man who lacks gray hair  is  as fleeting as the  mist!


<verse eid="ECC 11:10"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 11"/>
  <chapter number="12" style="c" sid="ECC 12"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ECC 12:1"/>Therefore, while you are young,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:1">live  without forgetting God who gave you your life.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 12:1">Do this before old age makes  life difficult and painful.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:1">At that time, you might say,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:1">“I no longer enjoy being alive.”
<verse eid="ECC 12:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ECC 12:2"/>Do this before the light from the sun, moon, and stars fades out
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:2">and the clouds disappear, having given all their rain.
<verse eid="ECC 12:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ECC 12:3"/>At that time, your arms that stand guard at your house will shake.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:3">The men who had strength will weaken.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:3">The grinders who mill grain will cease their task because too few of them remain.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:3">People looking out from their windows will see darkness.
<verse eid="ECC 12:3"/></para>
  <para style="q2"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ECC 12:4"/>Everybody will close the doors of their houses.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 12:4">The sound of people grinding grain will be too faint to hear.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:4">People will awaken to the crowing of birds.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:4">People will bow down  as they sing mournful songs.
<verse eid="ECC 12:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ECC 12:5"/>People will even fear high places.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:5">It will be dangerous to walk the roads.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:5">The flowers of almond trees will become white as they ripen.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:5">Grasshoppers will drag themselves on the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:5">The caperberry will fail to heal.
</para>
  <para style="q3" vid="ECC 12:5">This is because people are dying.
</para>
  <para style="q3" vid="ECC 12:5">Their relatives bury their bodies,
</para>
  <para style="q3" vid="ECC 12:5">and people circle the streets as they grieve.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 12:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ECC 12:6"/>Remember God before your life ends—
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:6">like a silver chain that snaps
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:6">or a golden bowl that falls and breaks;
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:6">like a jug that shatters against a well,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:6">or the wheel of a reservoir that cracks.
<verse eid="ECC 12:6"/></para>
  <para style="q2"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ECC 12:7"/>At that time, our bodies will decay and become dirt again,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:7">and our spirits will return to God who gave them.

<verse eid="ECC 12:7"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ECC 12:8"/>So, Qoheleth said: “All of life is as temporary and insubstantial as the mist of my breath! Everything is emptiness!”

<verse eid="ECC 12:8"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ECC 12:9"/>So, not only did people recognize that Qoheleth was a wise man, but he also taught his fellow Israelites many truths. He studied and considered many things during his life and wrote down many of the proverbs that he had collected.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 12:9"/><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ECC 12:10"/>Qoheleth labored so that he would only use suitable words which were also pleasant. The words that he wrote are reliable and true.

<verse eid="ECC 12:10"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ECC 12:11"/>Wise people teach us in a way that provokes a change in one’s behavior, just like the sharp tools that people use to spur large animals to move. The written collections of the things that wise people have taught are as enduring as nails that someone drives into wood. God is the one who gives these wise insights.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 12:11"/><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ECC 12:12"/>However, my child, understand that you will discover nothing profitable by listening to the teachings of so-called wise people. People will never stop writing books, and attempting to study them all will exhaust you.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="ECC 12:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ECC 12:13"/>This is the last of Qoheleth’s lectures,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:13">now that you’ve heard everything:
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 12:13">Fearfully submit yourself to God,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:13">so that you live your life the way he asks.
</para>
  <para style="q1" vid="ECC 12:13">Do this because this
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:13">is the duty of every human!
<verse eid="ECC 12:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ECC 12:14"/>This is because God will judge
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:14">everything that humans have  done,
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:14">even things that people have done secretly—
</para>
  <para style="q2" vid="ECC 12:14">good decisions and evil decisions alike.
<verse eid="ECC 12:14"/></para>
  <chapter eid="ECC 12"/>
</usx>
